Treat a backslash in an httpd request path as a separator on Windows.
httpd removes ".." components and matches password lines, subdirectory
config files and the cgi-bin/ prefix by looking for '/' alone, but
Windows also takes '\' as a path separator. A request for
/..\..\file.txt or /..%5C..%5Cfile.txt read files outside the home
directory, /adm\secret.htm skipped the password that /adm/secret.htm
needed, /sub\file.txt ignored sub/httpd.conf, and /cgi-bin%5Cx.cgi sent
the source of the program.

Turn backslashes into slashes once the path is decoded, so that every
later check sees the separators that Windows will.

---
 networking/httpd.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/networking/httpd.c
+++ b/networking/httpd.c
@@ -2716,6 +2716,10 @@
 		/* '/' or NUL is encoded */
 		send_headers_and_exit(HTTP_NOT_FOUND);
 	}
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+	/* Windows takes a backslash as a path separator too */
+	bs_to_slash(urlcopy);
+#endif
 
 	/* Canonicalize path */
